      {
        "case_id": "gp007",
        "category": "golden",
        "question": "What is the formula for scaled dot-product attention, and how does it compare to additive attention?",
        "answer": "- Scaled dot‑product attention computes the dot products of queries and keys, divides each by √dₖ, applies a softmax to obtain weights, and then multiplies by the values. In matrix form: Attention(Q, K, V) = softmax(Q Kᵀ / √dₖ) V. [chunk_1]\n- The compatibility function in additive attention is computed by a feed‑forward network with a single hidden layer. [chunk_1]\n- Dot‑product attention is identical to the scaled version except for the scaling factor 1/√dₖ. [chunk_1]\n- Dot‑product attention is faster and more space‑efficient in practice because it can be implemented with highly optimized matrix‑multiplication code. [chunk_2][chunk_1]\n- For small values of dₖ the two mechanisms perform similarly. [chunk_2]\n- For larger dₖ, additive attention outperforms dot‑product attention without scaling. [chunk_2]\n- Scaling the dot products by 1/√dₖ counteracts large magnitudes that push the softmax into regions with very small gradients. [chunk_2]",
